# klause working notes

Kotlin Multiplatform constraint solver.

## Gradle modules and publishing

- Do not expand the set of Maven Central published modules or artifacts because of
  Maven Central publishing limits. Keep published library implementation within the
  existing published modules.
- Additional non-published Gradle modules are allowed, such as `klause-bench`.
  Explicitly disable publication for them (`eignexPublish { publish.set(false) }`).

## Allowed languages

- Kotlin for all solver, library, CLI and bench code; Gradle Kotlin DSL for builds.
- Bash for glue scripts that chain standard tools (`curl`, `tar`, `jq`, `bc`, `sha256sum`), under
  a module's `scripts/`. Prefer a short script to a new `klause-bench`
  subcommand, and delete an unused tool instead of porting it.
- MiniZinc for the `klause-mzn-lib` library and test models.
- No Dockerfiles and no reference-solver code: klause-lab runs the reference solvers and owns their
  images. klause-bench only selects problems and resolves their files (`select files=true`).
- No Python anywhere: not in sources, tests, resources, Gradle tasks or CI.

## PR validation

Opening and pushing a PR does not require a local `./gradlew check lintDocs` gate.
Use GitHub CI for the full gate; targeted local tests during development are optional.
Record skipped or interrupted local validation accurately in the PR. Check CI before
reporting the PR as green, and fix failures through follow-up commits.

When running the full gate locally:

- check runs tests and some light checks.
- lintDocs runs detekt and dokka linting.
- Do NOT add `--rerun-tasks`. detekt re-analyses new and edited files on its own; the flag only
  forces the whole graph to redo work the cache already has.
- An idle-box gate is ~4 minutes, over half of it detekt. Batch every edit and gate once at the end
  — work that arrives in pieces otherwise buys a gate per piece, and no edit can overlap a running
  gate.
- detekt and dokka run on alpha analysis engines. Two crash shapes look alike and are not:
  - **It names one of your files.** `No receiver found in qualified expression`, or an offsets error
    like `Range [0, 27) out of bounds for length 4`, means the file changed under the analysis. Usually
    detekt's own `autoCorrect` resorting that file's imports mid-run, so a later rule reads a stale
    context. Real and repeatable: run detekt, commit the autocorrection it writes, and it goes. Editing
    a source file while the gate runs does the same thing, so don't. Reproducing it needs the whole
    `check` graph — a bare detekt run has no binding context, so the rule that crashes never fires.
  - **No file of yours appears in it.** `AsyncExecutionService.getService must not return null`, with
    every stack frame inside the Kotlin analysis API, is a flake: re-run before investigating.
  A dokka `Error in translating of symbol` can be either; one on an untouched file was a real broken
  link. Check the symbol it names first. A finding that names your file and a rule is always genuine.

## Tests

- Unit tests and their resource/setup tasks must not make network requests or provision external tools.
  Use local deterministic fixtures or fakes; external-reference integration runs belong in klause-lab.
  Ordinary Gradle dependency resolution is separate from test execution.
- Test names within backticks, plain ascii, describing the behavior under test.
- Class almost always named after the file under test (`<FileUnderTest>Test`).
- One observable behavior per test; assert behavior, not implementation. Follow arrange → act → assert.
- Mirror existing tests' framework, layout, and assertion style.
- Reuse existing fixtures/helpers; don't add new ones unless asked.
- Use the parameterized form for variants of the same behavior; separate tests for semantically different ones.
- Keep comments minimal and let the test name carry the intent.
- No JVM test over 300ms.

## Bench

Read `klause-bench/README.md` before benchmarking. In short:

- Experiments, A/B comparisons, reference runs, witness checks and profiling go through eignex-lab (`deploy/lab`).
- Use the authenticated HTTP API at `https://lab.eignex.com`. SSH access, host discovery and lab administration
  belong to agents explicitly working on eignex-lab operations; send concrete operational dependencies to that owner.
- A normal A/B row runs all arms for one workload, seed and repetition on the same machine in one worker attempt.
  Different rows, preparation and replacement attempts may use different compatible machines. Keep automatic
  placement; do not pin the whole job or reject completed pairs merely because the job's node changed.
- klause-bench provides what the lab calls: `select` names problems and resolves their files, `solve-one` solves one
  problem with klause-cli and writes its record, and the `bench.profile` mains run as `lab profile` jobs.
- `solve-one` runs the installed CLI as a subprocess, so rebuild it with `:klause-cli:installJvmDist` first.
  `installDist` is a no-op that serves a stale binary, so suspect it when a bench result contradicts a passing test.

## Style

- Prefer clear naming and types over comments. Comments explain *why* (soundness, perf, lifecycle) and never
  restate the code. Never narrate changes, avoid "now/no longer/previously", and don't cite issues. Keep `TODO`/`FIXME`.
- KDoc on public API only. Use functional notation for math (`succ(i)`, not `succ[i]`); use `[Symbol]` for links.
- Single-line conventional commits, no scope, no body/trailers:
  `feat: `, `fix: `, `chore: `, `refactor: `, `docs: `, `test: `.
- PR body: follow `.github/pull_request_template.md`, with `Closes #NN` on its own line at the top.
- Issues: follow `.github/issue_template.md`. Keep them short, a few plain sentences,
  and link code/PRs instead of pasting.

## Documentation

- `docs/` is a book about the Kotlin code: `docs/README.md` is the table of contents and chapters are
  numbered files (`NN-topic.md`), ordered so every concept is introduced before it is used. A new
  chapter goes after its prerequisites; renumber the files after it and update the contents and
  navigation. User documentation lives at `https://eignex.com/docs/klause/`.
- Read the owning chapter before changing a subsystem, and update it in the same change when
  architecture, ownership, dependencies, invariants, result semantics, resources or workflows change.
  Describe only the current system: no history, plans or comparisons with other solvers.
- Keep chapters tight and peers balanced: each theory, format, kernel or worker kind gets about as
  much text as its peers. Mechanism detail belongs in the owning chapter, API detail in KDoc.
- `docs/` excludes benchmarking, measurement, scripts, fixture provenance, wrapper setup and process.
  Benchmark usage lives in `klause-bench/README.md`, limited to running the bench and a pointer to
  eignex-lab. Keep experiment results and design-evidence summaries out of repository documentation.
  Fixture provenance and licences live in READMEs beside the fixtures, MiniZinc setup in
  `klause-mzn-lib/README.md`. Other READMEs are short entry points.
- Never commit campaign archives, raw records, payloads or one-off scripts, compressed or not. Keep
  them in `klause-evidence/campaigns/<name>/` beside the primary checkout (shared by all worktrees),
  promote reusable tools and fixtures into `klause-bench`, and delete bundles once their conclusions
  have landed.
- Keep `.github` to build/release workflows and issue/PR templates; no campaign or benchmark jobs.

## Solver boundaries

- `ir` owns engine-neutral literals, variable descriptions, remapping, and structural keys. It does not
  depend on engines, compilation, or input formats.
- `lowering` owns generic Boolean and constraint encodings shared by schema compilation and input formats.
  It does not depend on either caller. Input formats parse and lower source syntax; they are not compiler
  dependencies.
- `propagation` owns finite-domain state, explanations, and incremental propagators. Its difference
  system follows the trail and can pin literals back into the propagation fixpoint.
- `arithmetic.difference` owns immutable difference-constraint representation, fragment extraction,
  and non-incremental graph operations. It must not depend on propagation state.
- `simplex.exact` owns reusable exact rational arithmetic and feasibility simplex primitives.
- `lp.engine` owns solving an `LpModel` and certifying the result: the simplex, its basis, and the exact
  dual/Farkas certificates. It may depend only on `util`, `simplex.exact`, `lp.lattice`, and `simplex.basis` — never on
  factors, propagation, presolve, search, or the solver model. That closed list is what keeps the engine
  swappable, so widening it is the thing to refuse.
- `lp.lattice` owns exact integer matrix reduction (echelon, Hermite, unimodular transforms) and the
  bounds a triangular block implies. It is a leaf: `util` and nothing else.
- `lp` and its remaining subpackages own the model-facing half: relaxation assembly from factors,
  separators, bounding policy, open-model reasoning. Dependencies point at `lp.engine`, never back.
- `bound` owns the combinatorial bounds a CP constraint admits on its own: Lagrangian duals over
  AllDifferent and multi-knapsack structure, energetic and flow feasibility over `Cumulative`. It
  reads factors, `ir`, `solver.objective`, and propagation state, and may read `lp.relaxation`
  views — never `lp.engine`. Needing the simplex is what puts a bound under `lp` instead.
- `theory.difference`, `theory.lia`, and `theory.qflra` decide complete open-model fragments. They must
  not materialize finite CP domains.
- `solver.pipeline` selects an open theory once and exposes one verdict surface. Frontends render
  assignments; they do not select or invoke concrete theories.
- Dependencies point from policy to mechanism: pipeline and theories may depend on solver model types
  and arithmetic kernels; propagation may depend on immutable arithmetic representations; arithmetic
  kernels do not depend on propagation or search.
